Date: Thu, 07 Oct 93 20:23:45 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: free will

I've thought of a fourth definition of free-will which weighs in between
my first and third definitions -- probably a rehash of the second.  So
we could call this free-will(2):

"An individual acting under his/her/its own free will, is able to use
the reasoning ability of the brain in order to weigh up the pros and
cons of alternative actions, and after due deliberation select that
which appears to return the highest value at a given micro-instant of
time, as judged according to current values, beliefs and preferences.
The individual is also free to learn from past experiences and future
simulations; hence current values, beliefs, preferences, and even goals
are not stagnant but may be transformed in the light of new evidence
and computations.  In other words, the algorithms are not constant."

In some ways this goes further than definition one (illusion of
free-will), but at other times definition one isn't included in def.
two.  You could be ordered to dig your own grave at gunpoint, for 
instance, and still make the decision to buy a little more time.   

Or def. one might be satisfied but not def. two.  Taking drugs for
example could temporarily interfere with the ability to arrive at the
correct decision, and hence free-will.  Free-will wouldn't be a Boolean
quality, a thing that you either had or hadn't; its degree would vary
from one moment to the next.  The will would be one factor involved in
the decision making process, having ultimate control in fine-tuning the
behaviour; however environment and knowledge of bodily states via nerves
would be other co-factors, a bit like health is partly determined
genetically, partly by lifestyle, and by environment. 

John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us> writes:

>Not that it matters but I would be astounded if all human
>action is predetermined at the physical level, Quantum mechanics
>almost certainly throws in some randomness but even if it didn't
>by your first definition, the one I like, we would still have
>Free Will, and by the way, as illusions go there is nothing
>"mere" about it. 

We agree on the principle of downward causation, where a higher level
structure like the brain can be considered as the proximate cause.  The
next question is whether the conscious mind has properties above that
of an everyday data processing system -- see later.
	 
>	>There is however the possibility that the brain is actively
>	>involved in selecting behaviour, and under conscious control of
>	>the individual.  If this is so, it can be thought
>	>of as a second form of will.
	 
>I don't understand this definition. Of my own free will, I
>consciously decide to go to a restaurant. Why? Because I want
>to. Why ? Because I want to eat. Why? Because I'm hungry? Why ?
>Because lack of food triggered nerve impulses  in my stomach ,

Still, decisions are being made constantly -- you decide the exact
moment to travel to the restaurant (depending on whatever else you're
doing at the time), which one to go to, how to get there, what to eat
etc.  Although the hunger feelings may act as a trigger you're still
free to ignore them for one hour / three hours, and then other
rational decisions are arrived at to fine-tune all the events much
further.  Consciousness, intelligence and personal preferences come
into play.

>The reason we think we do something may not be the real reason
>we do it. Some drugs have the embarrassing side affect of making
>us irritable.  [...]
>Did the drug make me loose my free will? The world is full of
>drugs, some of the most potent  ones made by my own body.  

I'd say that the drug would have reduced the ability to reason, and
to objectively weigh up the pros and cons of possible actions.  The 
benefit of free-will is in being able to select the best action in
the circumstances, so this benefit has been reduced or lost.  And if
people commit crimes because they're on drugs, or they claim they
can't control themselves because of their "hormones" or something, I
don't think this should be an acceptable "excuse".

>I don't see the connection but the Everett many worlds
>interpretation is not something I have a deep understanding of
>so I may be missing something.

This is a topic I'd like to bring up a little later.  Mike Price
mentioned a thread from a couple of months back; I noticed a few folk
were recommending various works of fiction, but not much substance on
MWI/MMI.  Later I'll do a brief summary of how I see the differences 
and see what the reactions and implications are.

>	 >according to epiphenomenalism, mental experiences are merely
>	 >a byproduct of the brain, and serve no purpose.  So the                
>	 >evolution of consciousness could not be explained in
>	 >Darwinian terms.  It's much more likely that consciousness arose
>	 >because it served an excellent purpose. Those
>	 >animals that had it enjoyed a selective advantage over those
>	 >that didn't.
	  
>I agree with you . Intelligence without consciousness is
>impossible otherwise genetic drift would have eliminated  all
>consciousness long ago and I am certain that at least one
>instance of it still exists. That's why the Turing test works.
>You gave me a lot of ideas to chew on ,Thanks.

Intelligence without consciousness wouldn't have evolved, but we should 
treat the two separately.  A chess playing program might perform very
well, yet have no conception of the meaning behind the numbers it is
manipulating, and would not even know what a chess set looks like.  On
the other hand, you could have a raving loony who at least has enough
awareness to avoid bumping into things.  Although this wouldn't count
as a particularly good consciousness, as the relationship between
concepts might be very jumbled.

>	 >Although you would not expect to find any violation of
>	 >natural law through observation of any level, it's better to
>	 >think of the highest level of the hierarchy as the
>	 >cause of what's happening.  What we have is an example of
>	 >downward causation.
	 
>Yes I agree. To say that the atoms in my hand moved because I
>wanted to scratch my nose is meaningful, useful, and probably
>even  true .But it still doesn't answer the question "why did I
>want to scratch my nose?".
	
The nose-scratching is a rather trivial choice as the stakes
involved are very low.  Where the potential rewards and risks of
an action are much higher, a greater level of reasoning would be
allocated.
 
	 >I'll wager that if a high-level function required the
	 >particle to select a specific option in order to make things
	 >work at the higher level, the particle would
	 >"just happen" to select that option.
	 
>It seems to me that this would be very easy to test. The fact
>that I have not heard of any reports of this interesting
>phenomenon leads me to three possibilities  1) I don't read the
>right publications  2) Experimental Physics has been asleep at
>the switch for 80 years  3) The phenomenon does not exist.

If you accept the principle of downward causation then the high-level
function must be effecting causes on the particles.  But measuring
the position and momenta of all particles in the brain and relating to
mental experiences would be something beyond having to evaluate a 
spreadsheet package by watching pulses on the buses. 

>I was surprised that you disagree with the statement " There is
>no qualitative distinction between a massively parallel
>transputer and a single CPU serial computer " Alan Turing proved
>that over 50 years ago.
		       
It's true that Turing showed that a Universal Turing machine can do any
computation that a serial or parallel computer can do (although he also
showed that some problems are non-computable).  For any well-defined
computational procedures you can simulate the machine by reproducing
its input / output relationship, but for our example of a conscious mind
this is only useful if we presuppose that all the capabilities of a
self-conscious intelligent being can be reduced to an algorithm, and
there are no additional non-algorithmic properties facilitated by
parallel processing.  An algorithmic computer might not be truly
intelligent, lacking free-will, and ripe for enslavement with no risk
of mutiny. 

A computer cannot carry out some mathematical proofs that are just
'child's play'.  It can't ever prove that A * B = B * A whatever the
variable values, for instance.  It can keep on trying to falsify this,
and would soon induce a conclusion with a probability almost equal to
one.  But we can _see_ the proof!

    *  *  *  *                   *  *  *
    *  *  *  *  A            B   *  *  *
    *  *  *  *                   *  *  *
         B                       *  *  *
                                    A

We can see that whatever the size of the rectangle, you can just rotate
it, and the product of A and B is the same.  This seems to be an
example of a direct insight of part of Popper's World Three, or Plato's
eternal world of universals and mathematical truths -- memes that were
discovered rather than invented. 

Well, the conscious mind clearly has abilities beyond that of an
unconscious data processing machine.  Otherwise subjective mental
events wouldn't have evolved, and it has to have control in order
to have any causal significance.  If consciousness was just an
epiphenomenon, philosophers' zombies would have no trouble passing
the Turing test.

Apart from sensory awareness of the external world and of internal
states, it's the associations that are important.  If I experience a
feeling of green it's more meaningful to me than to some mechanical
machine that was simply designed to respond to a specific wavelength
of light.  I associate green with a visual rather than any other
sensory experience, I associate green with fields, trees, grass, and
associate fields with particular places, other concepts....  If you
had enough ram and tried to multitask the operations on a serial machine,
each operation would just be a meaningless manipulation of numbers and
symbols, and every time a conditional branch required knowledge of what
the symbols represented it would not be able to get instant information
from hard-wired interconnections but would have to do a lot of searching.
I've concluded that it might just work, although I'd be concerned that
there were very subtle differences in operation compared to the true
entity. 

Some time I intend to have myself uploaded, and I'm not quite convinced
that something as personal as an individual's consciousness could be
run or "simulated" on just any general purpose hardware with a few 
suitable kludges (although I think it would be silly to assume that
other hardware platforms are impossible).  I hope it is as easy as
some suggest -- I don't want to wake up and find that my bath's full
of little green men with antennae, who have come to experiment on me! 

