Not at all. In fact this conversation points up an important fact
about security: it's not an absolute good and treating it as such
leads to extreme-paranoid-survivalist stances that will conflict with
your enjoyment of life. You assess the level of threat in your chosen
environments, and invest enough in security to deal with that level,
and toss in a few % safety margin like a good engineer.

Exactly the same thing is true of data security. It has a cost, which
should be limited by the value of the data being protected. It's just
that when we deal in lives, we get mystical and start assigning values
like \$$\Aleph_0$ ...
