Extropians,

Timothy C. May said:

    . . . Indeed, use of untraceable escrow services will
    make the market for killings-for-hire much more liquid,
    so to speak.

To which Perry Metzger replied:

    Wouldn't it be easy to catch such services with sting
    operations? I still fail to see what the problem is.

I know Tim likes to make us think by stating his cases in the
most extreme terms.  Here though, I just can't buy the premise.
Crypto anarchy doesn't give the criminals any advantage over the
non-criminal, on sum.  When we look at the world's biggest
criminals (i.e., nation states), Tim has no problem seeing how
individuals come out ahead.  So it is too, with garden variety
criminals.

We don't need the government to have a sting operation.  That's
what private insurance companies/defense agencies are for.  You
can bet, they will do everything in its power to keep from having
to pay off on my extortion insurance.  And "anything" might
include some pretty rough crypto anarchy tricks of its own--up to
and including some variation of the "Coca Cola gambit."

This story has circulated within the mercenary and covert
operations community for many years.  Is it true?  I don't know,
but it is still instructive.  In the '70s, a number of large
American companies with operations in Latin America were preyed
upon by various terrorists groups.  The terrorists were
kidnapping American executives on a regular basis.  They then
extorted money, political statements or whatever out of the
executives' companies.  Often, even though the ransom was paid,
the executive was killed anyway.

When an executive of Coca Cola was finally abducted, Coke
supposedly decided to handle things differently.  The rumor is
they hired a private group which specialized in anti-terrorist
operations and gave them carte blanche.  The group researched the
terrorists.  They found out they were a nominally Communist
guerrilla band that operated out of a particular area of the
country.  The anti-terrorists then went to that area a did
further research on the guerrillas, their haunts, their supply
lines, their local supporters, etc.

Arrangements were made to rendezvous with the terrorists in order
to give them the ransom money they demanded.  Instead if a
representative of Coca Cola, however, one of the anti-terrorists
went to the meeting.  When he met the guerrillas' representative,
he opened his briefcase and removed a thick stack of photographs
and list of names and addresses.  The photos were of every man,
woman, child, pet and farm animal that lived in the area from
which the guerrillas operated.  Drawn over each picture was a
stylized gun sight.  The anti-terrorist left without a further
word.  Within days, the executive was returned, and no one else
from Coca Cola was ever abducted again.

Okay, so how does this apply to crypto anarchy?  Yes, the
guerrillas could more easily hide behind anonymity using crypto
techniques, but "more easily" doesn't mean "absolutely safely."
Even the most secure system isn't foolproof.  How much would any
criminal be willing to risk if such a massive (and immoral)
response were even remotely possible?

Crypto anarchy makes some crimes marginally easier to commit.  It
also makes horrendous, extra-legal punishments possible as well.
The left hand giveth; the right hand taketh away.  I'd gladly
dump the world we have today, and opt for crypto anarchy--the bad
and the good--in a heartbeat.

