Extropians,

Sean Gladfelter gave us several contradictory views on armed
self-defense:

    Keeping a gun or guns in the home for protection is a
    good idea which I do condone.  [Gee thanks, Sean.]

    . . .  if you follow the guidelines given in any
    hunter's safety course.

Bad idea.  Guns for self-defense and guns for hunting have very
different missions.  It follows that how you store them will also
depend upon their mission.  Sean goes on the tell how to make
your defense gun useless for defense (e.g., keep the ammo away
from the gun, lock everything up).  With a weapon owned only for
hunting, you may have the luxury of making it absolutely safe at
home.  If you have a weapon for defense, though, you have to
assume some risk in having it readily available for use.

    . . . the burglar does not know whether or not the gun
    is loaded or unloaded when he's looking down the barrel.

Sean, get a gun, look down the barrel (using a mirror isn't a bad
idea), then come back and tell us what you saw.  Revolvers are
obvious, but even with autoloaders you can usually see the round.
(God, it looks BIG!)

    If you gun down a burglar, keep in mind you HAVE
    COMMITTED A CRIME, unless it's proven beyond a
    reasonable doubt that you were in jeopardy of life or
    limb.

Now it's been some time since I was in law school, but that's not
the way I remember it.  In the theoretical world, the prosecution
has the burden of proof to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that
the shooter committed a crime, not the other way 'round.  In the
real world, it's even better.  In almost every jurisdiction, no
prosecutor in his/her right mind will charge the home defender
with anything.  Even the cops will bend over backwards to
characterize the shooting as "righteous."  In may western states,
such as California, the law is written such that there is a
*presumption* of the threat of death or great bodily harm
whenever a burglar is in your house.  Trust me on this.  You're a
criminal justice major.  I'm a lawyer.  I out-rank you. :-)

    There are many non-lethal, and very effective weapons on
    the market, that require no permit to carry.  Mace, stun
    guns, key chain weapons, and even crowd control grenades
    can be purchased by the average citizen (at least in
    PA).

This is utter nonsense.  None of the named weapons are
non-lethal, and none of them are very effective.  All of them
have killed people under some circumstances, yet they don't even
come close to the stopping power of a gun.  I am so confidant of
that last statement, that I will gladly wager $200 that I can
remove two $100 bills from your shirt pocket if your only means
of stopping me is mace or a stun gun.  For five $100 dollar bills
I'll risk lacerations from your key chain, and for an even grand
I'll play hot potato with your crowd control grenades.  (You use
grenades in Pennsylvania to thwart burglars?)  Any takers?

